PNT.registerModule({"id": "m06", "number": 6, "title": "PNT Threats and Vulnerabilities", "subtitle": "", "version": "0.1", "objectives": ["Distinguish denial, degradation and deception of PNT services.", "Explain jamming at a systems-engineering level and why GNSS is susceptible to radio-frequency interference.", "Explain spoofing and distinguish it from ordinary measurement error.", "Explain meaconing as the rebroadcast or delay of legitimate navigation signals.", "Recognise accidental interference, equipment failure and environmental disruption as PNT threats.", "Explain how cyber and data-layer compromise can affect PNT without directly attacking the radio signal.", "Describe the effects of space weather on GNSS performance and availability.", "Identify common-mode vulnerabilities across nominally redundant PNT sources.", "Recognise observable indicators that may suggest PNT degradation or manipulation.", "Apply a risk lifecycle of identify, protect, detect, respond and recover to PNT-dependent systems.", "Distinguish threat, vulnerability, failure mechanism, harm and operational consequence when analysing PNT risk.", "Apply a complete-system threat model across source, signal/data, receiver, processing, interfaces, dependencies and consuming application.", "Recognise supply-chain and third-party service dependencies as part of the PNT attack and failure surface.", "Explain why threat attribution is secondary to timely detection of loss of PNT trust and safe operational response."], "lessons": [{"id": "when-pnt-cannot-be-trusted", "title": "When PNT Cannot Be Trusted", "html": "<p>Previous modules established how PNT is produced, why GNSS measurements contain errors, how augmentation improves performance and how alternative sources can provide diversity. We now examine what happens when those sources are disrupted, manipulated or fail.</p>\n<p>PNT threats are not limited to military electronic warfare. A system can be affected by deliberate jamming or spoofing, accidental radio-frequency interference, equipment faults, bad data, cyber compromise, supply-chain weaknesses, environmental effects, space weather, infrastructure outages or common-mode dependencies. The engineering task is to understand not only the initiating event, but which PNT asset is affected, what harm reaches the mission, how quickly it can be detected and what evidence is available to support a safe response.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-principle\"><p class=\"callout-title\">The central question</p><div class=\"callout-body\">How can a PNT system become unavailable, degraded or convincingly wrong, and how can the system or operator recognise that trust has been lost?</div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"m06-learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Distinguish denial, degradation and deception of PNT services.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain jamming at a systems-engineering level and why GNSS is susceptible to radio-frequency interference.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain spoofing and distinguish it from ordinary measurement error.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain meaconing as the rebroadcast or delay of legitimate navigation signals.</p></li>\n<li><p>Recognise accidental interference, equipment failure and environmental disruption as PNT threats.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain how cyber and data-layer compromise can affect PNT without directly attacking the radio signal.</p></li>\n<li><p>Describe the effects of space weather on GNSS performance and availability.</p></li>\n<li><p>Identify common-mode vulnerabilities across nominally redundant PNT sources.</p></li>\n<li><p>Recognise observable indicators that may suggest PNT degradation or manipulation.</p></li>\n<li><p>Apply a risk lifecycle of identify, protect, detect, respond and recover to PNT-dependent systems.</p></li>\n<li><p>Distinguish threat, vulnerability, failure mechanism, harm and operational consequence when analysing PNT risk.</p></li>\n<li><p>Apply a complete-system threat model across source, signal/data, receiver, processing, interfaces, dependencies and consuming application.</p></li>\n<li><p>Recognise supply-chain and third-party service dependencies as part of the PNT attack and failure surface.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain why threat attribution is secondary to timely detection of loss of PNT trust and safe operational response.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"m06-scope-and-safety\">Scope and safety</h2>\n<p>This module teaches threat recognition, engineering consequences, defensive monitoring and assurance. It does not provide instructions for constructing jammers, generating counterfeit GNSS signals or defeating PNT systems.</p>"}, {"id": "threat-vulnerability-harm-and-mission-consequence", "title": "Threat, Vulnerability, Harm and Mission Consequence", "html": "<p>A mature PNT threat assessment separates the cause from the effect. A threat or adverse condition acts on a vulnerability in an asset. That interaction can create harm to the PNT service, which then creates an operational consequence for the consuming system. This prevents the analysis from becoming a list of technologies or hostile techniques without explaining why they matter.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Concept</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Meaning in this module</strong></th>\n<th><strong>PNT example</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Engineering question</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Threat / adverse condition</td>\n<td>Something capable of causing disruption or manipulation.</td>\n<td>RF interference, software compromise, space weather.</td>\n<td>What can act on the system?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Vulnerability</td>\n<td>A weakness or dependency that allows the threat to cause harm.</td>\n<td>Single antenna, unauthenticated interface, shared power.</td>\n<td>Why can the threat affect us?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>PNT harm</td>\n<td>Loss of required PNT quality or trust.</td>\n<td>Position unavailable, time biased, integrity lost.</td>\n<td>What changes in the PNT service?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Mission consequence</td>\n<td>Effect on the user or operational capability.</td>\n<td>Autonomy restricted, synchronisation lost, unsafe navigation.</td>\n<td>Why does the harm matter?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td colspan=\"4\"><strong>Engineer insight<br/>\n</strong>Threat modelling should be mission-led. Start with the PNT-dependent capability and its tolerances, then work backwards to the threats and vulnerabilities capable of violating them. 'Jamming is a threat' is incomplete unless the affected service, required performance and operational consequence are understood.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "three-useful-failure-states", "title": "Three Useful Failure States", "html": "<p>Before naming individual threats, classify the effect on the user. This keeps the analysis focused on operational consequence rather than threat terminology.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>State</strong></th>\n<th><strong>What the user experiences</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Example consequence</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Denied / unavailable</td>\n<td>Required PNT information cannot be obtained.</td>\n<td>Navigation solution is lost; timing reference disappears.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Degraded</td>\n<td>PNT remains available but no longer meets required performance.</td>\n<td>Position error grows beyond operational tolerance; timing uncertainty increases.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Deceived / misleading</td>\n<td>PNT information appears usable but is materially wrong.</td>\n<td>Platform follows a false position/route; system timestamps events incorrectly without warning.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Why deception matters</p><div class=\"callout-body\">A clean loss of PNT can trigger a fallback mode. Convincing but incorrect PNT may continue to drive automation and human decisions. For many safety- and mission-critical systems, misleading information is therefore a particularly important integrity hazard.</div></aside>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Three Useful Failure States\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m06-image1.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>"}, {"id": "why-gnss-signals-are-vulnerable-to-interference", "title": "Why GNSS Signals Are Vulnerable to Interference", "html": "<p>GNSS signals travel thousands of kilometres from satellites and arrive at the Earth's surface at very low received power. A receiver must recover structured signals from a noisy radio-frequency environment.</p>\n<p>This makes GNSS highly useful but also means local radio-frequency energy can interfere with reception. Interference can be deliberate or accidental, narrowband or broadband, continuous or intermittent, localised or geographically widespread.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Important distinction</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Interference describes unwanted RF energy affecting reception. Jamming is deliberate interference intended to deny or degrade the service. Not every interference event is a hostile attack.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "jamming-denying-the-signal", "title": "Jamming: Denying the Signal", "html": "<p>Jamming is the deliberate transmission of interfering radio-frequency energy intended to prevent or degrade reception of a navigation signal. At the receiver, the practical effect is a reduced ability to acquire or track legitimate signals or form usable measurements.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Jamming: Denying the Signal\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m06-image2.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>\n<h2 id=\"m06-what-might-a-user-observe\">5.1 What might a user observe?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Loss of tracked satellites or repeated acquisition failures.</p></li>\n<li><p>Falling carrier-to-noise or other signal-quality indicators across several satellites.</p></li>\n<li><p>Sudden deterioration in position/timing availability.</p></li>\n<li><p>Receiver alarms or interference indicators, where supported.</p></li>\n<li><p>Multiple nearby systems losing GNSS at a similar time.</p></li>\n<li><p>An INS or other independent source continuing while GNSS measurements disappear.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-principle\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Detection principle</p><div class=\"callout-body\">A receiver losing one satellite may be normal. Many unrelated satellite signals degrading together can indicate a common RF problem. Detection relies on patterns and independent evidence, not one indicator in isolation.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "spoofing-deceiving-the-receiver", "title": "Spoofing: Deceiving the Receiver", "html": "<p>Spoofing is the transmission or presentation of false navigation information intended to cause a receiver or consuming system to calculate an incorrect PNT solution. Unlike simple jamming, the objective is deception rather than obvious loss of service.</p>\n<p>A spoofed solution may initially appear plausible. Position or time may diverge gradually, or false information may be constructed to resemble normal behaviour. This makes spoofing fundamentally an integrity problem.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><div class=\"callout-body\"><figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Spoofing: Deceiving the Receiver\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m06-image3.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"m06-possible-indicators-of-deception\">6.1 Possible indicators of deception</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>GNSS position disagrees with inertial, odometry, visual, radar, map or other independent information.</p></li>\n<li><p>Reported motion is inconsistent with platform dynamics or physical constraints.</p></li>\n<li><p>GNSS-derived time disagrees with an independent clock or network reference.</p></li>\n<li><p>Several satellite measurements change in an unusually correlated manner.</p></li>\n<li><p>Receiver signal/authentication or integrity indicators report anomalies.</p></li>\n<li><p>Position jumps or drifts in a way that cannot be explained by the operational environment.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Do not rely on one check</p><div class=\"callout-body\">A sophisticated integrity architecture uses multiple independent observations. A map constraint alone can be wrong; an INS can drift; a network clock can fail. Confidence comes from diverse evidence and explicit fault handling.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "meaconing-and-rebroadcast", "title": "Meaconing and Rebroadcast", "html": "<p>Meaconing is a form of navigation deception in which genuine navigation signals are received and then rebroadcast, commonly with delay or from another location. The content may originate from legitimate satellites, but the propagation context has been altered.</p>\n<p>For the victim receiver, delayed or displaced signals can create false apparent ranges or timing. This illustrates an important point: authentic source data does not automatically mean the received measurement is trustworthy.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Authentication has limits</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Signal or data authentication can help establish that information originated from an ynchroniz source. It does not, by itself, prove that the signal arrived through the expected path, at the expected time, without delay or rebroadcast.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "accidental-rf-interference", "title": "Accidental RF Interference", "html": "<p>Many PNT disruptions are not deliberate. Faulty electronics, poorly installed equipment, harmonics, adjacent-band emissions, radio systems and other electromagnetic sources can interfere with GNSS reception.</p>\n<p>The operational symptoms can resemble deliberate jamming. Engineering investigation should therefore separate observed effect from assumed intent.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Observation</strong></th>\n<th><strong>What it tells you</strong></th>\n<th><strong>What it does not prove</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>GNSS signal quality collapses locally</td>\n<td>An RF/environmental reception problem may exist</td>\n<td>That the interference is deliberate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Several receivers fail together</td>\n<td>A shared external/common dependency is plausible</td>\n<td>The exact source or mechanism.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Failure occurs near particular equipment</td>\n<td>Correlation worth investigating</td>\n<td>Causation without further evidence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Interference disappears when location changes</td>\n<td>Problem may be geographically local</td>\n<td>Whether it is accidental or hostile.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Good assurance language</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Say ‘GNSS interference was observed’ when that is what the evidence supports. Do not automatically label an unexplained RF event as jamming.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "cyber-and-data-layer-threats", "title": "Cyber and Data-Layer Threats", "html": "<p>PNT can be manipulated without attacking the satellite signal. Modern PNT solutions depend on software, firmware, correction services, configuration, networks, reference data and interfaces to consuming applications.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Attack / failure surface</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Potential PNT consequence</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Receiver firmware/software</td>\n<td>Incorrect processing, altered outputs, disabled monitoring or compromised configuration.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Correction service / augmentation data</td>\n<td>Incorrect corrections can bias a high-accuracy solution or cause loss of service.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Network time services</td>\n<td>False or delayed time can affect ynchronization and timestamps.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Reference-station infrastructure</td>\n<td>Bad coordinates/data can contaminate differential services.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Configuration / reference frame</td>\n<td>Incorrect datum, antenna offsets or settings can create systematic error.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Application interface</td>\n<td>Correct PNT can be misinterpreted, transformed incorrectly or associated with the wrong timestamp.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Credentials / service access</td>\n<td>Loss or compromise can interrupt or manipulate dependent augmentation services.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Cyber and Data-Layer Threats\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m06-image4.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">PNT assurance is cyber-physical</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Protecting the RF front end while ignoring software, data and network dependencies leaves major attack and failure paths unaddressed.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "space-weather-and-natural-effects", "title": "Space Weather and Natural Effects", "html": "<p>Solar activity can disturb the ionosphere and affect GNSS signal propagation. Severe space-weather events can increase measurement errors, create rapid ionospheric variations or contribute to loss of signal tracking, particularly in affected regions and conditions.</p>\n<p>Space weather is not malicious, but from the application's perspective the requirement is similar: detect when the primary PNT source no longer meets required performance and transition safely.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Threat versus hazard</p><div class=\"callout-body\">A resilient architecture should not depend on correctly attributing intent before protecting the mission. Natural, accidental and deliberate causes can produce similar operational effects.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "equipment-antenna-and-infrastructure-failure", "title": "Equipment, Antenna and Infrastructure Failure", "html": "<p>Not every PNT failure originates outside the system. Antenna damage, cable faults, power problems, oscillator failure, receiver defects, software updates, reference-station outages and network failures can all remove or corrupt PNT.</p>\n<p>Redundant receivers can still share the same antenna, power supply, software version, correction provider or network path. A design that appears redundant on a block diagram may therefore contain hidden single points of failure.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Equipment, Antenna and Infrastructure Failure\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m06-image5.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>"}, {"id": "common-mode-failure", "title": "Common-Mode Failure", "html": "<p>Common-mode failure occurs when one cause affects multiple supposedly redundant components or sources. PNT systems are particularly vulnerable to superficial redundancy because several sources may share RF spectrum, infrastructure, timing, software or environmental exposure.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Apparently diverse design</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Possible common-mode dependency</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>GPS + Galileo + BeiDou</td>\n<td>All require weak space-to-Earth GNSS RF reception and may share one antenna/receiver.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Two GNSS receivers</td>\n<td>Same antenna, power, software or physical location.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>GNSS + network timing</td>\n<td>Network timing source may itself be disciplined by GNSS.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>GNSS + LEO PNT</td>\n<td>LEO service may use GNSS-derived orbit/time or overlapping RF/infrastructure.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Two correction providers</td>\n<td>May share reference stations, telecoms carrier, cloud region or upstream products.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>INS + wheel odometry</td>\n<td>Both can be corrupted by incorrect nitialization or a faulty fused-state estimator.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Resilience question</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Do the sources merely have different names, or can they continue to provide trustworthy information when the same real-world failure occurs?</div></aside>"}, {"id": "supply-chain-and-third-party-dependencies", "title": "Supply Chain and Third-Party Dependencies", "html": "<p>PNT user equipment rarely operates in isolation. Receivers, antennas, firmware, correction services, cloud platforms, network links, reference stations, timing servers and integration software may come from different suppliers. A weakness, outage, counterfeit component, compromised update or undocumented upstream dependency can therefore affect PNT without any hostile action at the antenna.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Dependency</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Potential issue</strong></th>\n<th><strong>What to establish</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Hardware / firmware supplier</td>\n<td>Defect, malicious modification, unsupported component or common software baseline.</td>\n<td>Provenance, update process, known limitations, verification evidence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Correction / timing service</td>\n<td>Outage, corrupted data, shared upstream GNSS dependency.</td>\n<td>SLA, source provenance, independence, monitoring, fallback.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Network / cloud path</td>\n<td>Latency, loss, compromise or shared carrier/region.</td>\n<td>Alternate path, latency tolerance, security controls, outage behaviour.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Integrator / configuration</td>\n<td>Incorrect datum, antenna offset, time scale or interface mapping.</td>\n<td>Configuration baseline, review, test evidence and change control.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td colspan=\"3\"><strong>Assurance question<br/>\n</strong>When a supplier says a source is ‘independent’, ask what it depends on upstream. Independence has to survive the failure being analysed, not merely look different at the interface.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "detecting-loss-of-trust", "title": "Detecting Loss of Trust", "html": "<p>Detection is central to resilient PNT. A system should not only consume PNT; it should monitor whether the information remains plausible and suitable for the required operation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m06-receiver-level-monitoring\">14.1 Receiver-level monitoring</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Signal quality and tracking consistency.</p></li>\n<li><p>Satellite/measurement residuals and solution consistency.</p></li>\n<li><p>Receiver interference or spoofing indicators where available.</p></li>\n<li><p>Authentication features where supported.</p></li>\n<li><p>Solution status, protection levels or integrity flags.</p></li>\n<li><p>Unexpected changes in clock bias, position, velocity or uncertainty.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"m06-cross-source-monitoring\">14.2 Cross-source monitoring</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>GNSS versus INS propagated state.</p></li>\n<li><p>GNSS speed versus wheel/vehicle speed.</p></li>\n<li><p>GNSS altitude versus barometric or terrain constraints.</p></li>\n<li><p>GNSS position versus visual/radar/map matching.</p></li>\n<li><p>GNSS time versus independent oscillator, terrestrial or network time.</p></li>\n<li><p>Primary correction service versus independent reference or sanity checks.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Detecting Loss of Trust\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m06-image6.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">A vital interface requirement</p><div class=\"callout-body\">The application needs more than coordinates. It may need uncertainty, source status, age, integrity state and alerts so it can decide whether the PNT information is safe to use.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "govern-identify-protect-detect-respond-recover", "title": "Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, Recover", "html": "<p>The 2026 NIST Foundational PNT Profile aligns PNT risk management with Cybersecurity Framework 2.0. For engineering purposes, this adds two important ideas to the familiar protect-detect-respond-recover cycle: governance of PNT risk and explicit identification of dependencies, sources, requirements and threats. The result is a lifecycle that connects technical controls to organisational risk ownership and mission outcomes.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Recovery is not simply 'GNSS is back'</p><div class=\"callout-body\">After a suspected deception or data compromise, the system may need to re-establish a trustworthy state using independent information. Automatically trusting the first returning signal can preserve a corrupted solution.</div></aside>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Stage</strong></th>\n<th><strong>PNT engineering activity</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Govern</td>\n<td>Establish ownership, risk tolerance, roles, supplier responsibilities and decision authority for PNT risk.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Identify</td>\n<td>Map PNT dependencies, sources, data flows, operating/performance requirements, threats, vulnerabilities and consequences.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Protect</td>\n<td>Apply proportionate technical and procedural controls, secure configuration, source diversity and robust installation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Detect</td>\n<td>Continuously monitor signal/data quality, source agreement, integrity indicators, thresholds and operational plausibility.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Respond</td>\n<td>Reject or quarantine suspect information, notify downstream users, transition to alternatives or degraded modes and preserve evidence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Recover</td>\n<td>Revalidate sources, restore trusted state against defined criteria, acceptance-test restored service and feed lessons back into risk management.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td colspan=\"2\"><strong>Risk is dynamic<br/>\n</strong>NIST recommends reassessing PNT risk when equipment changes, threat likelihood changes, PNT dependency increases or recovery produces new lessons. A threat model should therefore be maintained, not treated as a one-off design document.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>For timing and recovery planning, useful objective measures include a maximum tolerable PNT error during holdover or degraded operation and a recovery time objective for restoring acceptable service. The exact metrics must be derived from the consuming mission, not copied from another sector.</p>"}, {"id": "graceful-degradation", "title": "Graceful Degradation", "html": "<p>A resilient system should have defined behaviour between 'fully operational' and 'failed'. Graceful degradation means retaining useful capability while explicitly reducing functionality or confidence as PNT quality deteriorates.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>State</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Illustrative behaviour</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Normal</td>\n<td>All required PNT performance achieved; full function enabled.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Degraded</td>\n<td>Primary source suspect/lost; alternative sources maintain reduced but acceptable capability.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Restricted</td>\n<td>Uncertainty exceeds normal requirement; application limits speed, autonomy, precision or available functions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Unavailable / safe state</td>\n<td>Required integrity cannot be maintained; affected function is inhibited or transferred to a safe/manual mode.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Recovery</td>\n<td>Sources are revalidated and the system transitions back under controlled criteria.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Requirement opportunity</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Define what the system must still do after 10 seconds, 1 minute, 10 minutes or an hour without its primary source. 'Resilient to GNSS loss' is not measurable until duration, performance and behaviour are specified.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "threat-modelling-a-pnt-system", "title": "Threat Modelling a PNT System", "html": "<p>Threat modelling should follow the complete PNT chain rather than focusing only on the antenna.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">SOURCE → SIGNAL / DATA → RECEIVER / SENSOR → PROCESSING → PNT SOLUTION → APPLICATION</p><div class=\"callout-body\"></div></aside>\n<p>For each stage, ask:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>What can fail naturally or accidentally?</p></li>\n<li><p>What can be denied?</p></li>\n<li><p>What can be manipulated or deceived?</p></li>\n<li><p>What dependencies are shared with other sources?</p></li>\n<li><p>What would the consuming application observe?</p></li>\n<li><p>How quickly could the failure be detected?</p></li>\n<li><p>What independent evidence is available?</p></li>\n<li><p>What fallback or degraded mode exists?</p></li>\n<li><p>How is trust re-established after recovery?</p></li>\n<li><p>What evidence demonstrates that the controls work?</p></li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "worked-scenario-maritime-gnss-interference", "title": "Worked Scenario: Maritime GNSS Interference", "html": "<p>A modern vessel's GNSS position begins to diverge from its expected route while the receiver continues to report a valid fix. This is not merely hypothetical: the Royal Institute of Navigation's 2026 maritime report documents operational GNSS interference, observed spoofing patterns, detection challenges and interconnected impacts across navigation, SOLAS, communications and other vessel systems. In the training scenario, reported speed remains plausible, so there is no obvious total loss of navigation.</p>\n<p>A resilient assessment does not immediately decide that spoofing is the cause. Instead, it compares GNSS against independent information: inertial/heading sensors, radar ranges, visual landmarks, chart constraints, speed through water and other available sources. It also checks receiver signal-quality and integrity indicators.</p>\n<p>If the sources disagree beyond expected uncertainty, GNSS should be treated as suspect. The navigation system or operator can reduce reliance on it, transition to alternative sources and preserve evidence for later diagnosis.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Lesson</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Detection of misleading PNT is fundamentally about inconsistency with independent truth or physics. Attribution of the cause can come later.</div></aside>\n<p>The wider lesson is dependency propagation. One corrupted GNSS input can be distributed to multiple bridge and communications systems, creating correlated symptoms that appear to be independent failures. When investigating an incident, map where the PNT data originated, how it was distributed and which systems transformed or rebroadcast it.</p>"}, {"id": "worked-scenario-timing-attack-or-failure", "title": "Worked Scenario: Timing Attack or Failure", "html": "<p>A data centre uses GNSS-disciplined clocks. One site's GNSS-derived time begins moving away from the rest of the network but remains internally stable.</p>\n<p>Possible causes include RF interference, spoofing, receiver fault, antenna/cable problems, bad configuration or a data/software issue. Independent network timing, local oscillator behaviour and cross-site comparisons can reveal the inconsistency.</p>\n<p>The system can reject the suspect reference, enter holdover or switch to an independent source while maintaining an explicit estimate of timing uncertainty.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Do not diagnose from the symptom alone</p><div class=\"callout-body\">A time offset does not prove spoofing. Good PNT monitoring separates detection, safe response and later root-cause attribution.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "speaking-like-a-pnt-engineer", "title": "Speaking Like a PNT Engineer", "html": "<p>When a supplier claims a solution is 'jam-resistant', 'spoof-proof' or 'assured PNT', useful questions include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>What threat model and interference conditions is the claim based on?</p></li>\n<li><p>Which PNT functions remain available and at what performance?</p></li>\n<li><p>Does the solution detect deception as well as denial?</p></li>\n<li><p>What independent sources are used for cross-checking?</p></li>\n<li><p>Are those sources genuinely independent of GNSS and of each other?</p></li>\n<li><p>What happens when sources disagree?</p></li>\n<li><p>What is the detection time for misleading information?</p></li>\n<li><p>What false-alarm and missed-detection behaviour has been demonstrated?</p></li>\n<li><p>What information is exposed to the application: uncertainty, integrity, source state and alerts?</p></li>\n<li><p>How long can degraded operation be sustained?</p></li>\n<li><p>How is a trusted state re-established after an incident?</p></li>\n<li><p>What representative test environment and evidence support the claim?</p></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Avoid absolute language</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Claims such as 'un-jammable' or 'spoof-proof' should be treated carefully. Engineering assurance should define the threat conditions, required performance and evidence rather than assuming absolute immunity.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "module-summary", "title": "Module Summary", "html": "<ul>\n<li><p>PNT can be denied, degraded or deceived; these states have different operational consequences.</p></li>\n<li><p>Jamming deliberately interferes with reception, while accidental RF interference can produce similar symptoms without hostile intent.</p></li>\n<li><p>Spoofing attempts to make false PNT information appear credible and is therefore an integrity threat.</p></li>\n<li><p>Meaconing shows that legitimate signal content can still produce misleading measurements when delayed or rebroadcast.</p></li>\n<li><p>PNT attack surfaces include RF, software, firmware, networks, correction services, configuration and application interfaces.</p></li>\n<li><p>Space weather, equipment faults and infrastructure outages must be considered alongside deliberate threats.</p></li>\n<li><p>Nominal redundancy can hide common-mode dependencies such as shared antennas, power, networks, software or upstream timing.</p></li>\n<li><p>Detection should combine receiver-level monitoring with independent cross-source and physical-consistency checks.</p></li>\n<li><p>PNT risk management should connect governance with identification, protection, detection, response and recovery.</p></li>\n<li><p>Graceful degradation and recovery criteria should be specified as measurable system behaviour, not vague claims of resilience.</p></li>\n<li><p>Threat analysis should connect adverse conditions and vulnerabilities to PNT harm and mission consequence, rather than stopping at threat names.</p></li>\n<li><p>Supply-chain, supplier and upstream service dependencies can create PNT vulnerabilities and common-mode failures outside the RF domain.</p></li>\n<li><p>PNT risk management benefits from explicit governance and continuous reassessment as systems, dependencies and threat conditions change.</p></li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "key-terms", "title": "Key Terms", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Term</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Working definition</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Jamming</td>\n<td>Deliberate RF interference intended to deny or degrade reception of a PNT signal.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Interference</td>\n<td>Unwanted RF energy that degrades reception; may be accidental or deliberate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Spoofing</td>\n<td>Deliberate provision of false or manipulated PNT information intended to deceive a receiver/system.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Meaconing</td>\n<td>Reception and rebroadcast/delay of legitimate navigation signals to create misleading observations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Denial</td>\n<td>State in which required PNT information is unavailable.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Degradation</td>\n<td>State in which PNT remains available but does not meet required performance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Deception</td>\n<td>State in which apparently usable PNT information is materially misleading.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Integrity monitoring</td>\n<td>Processes used to assess whether PNT information remains trustworthy for its intended use.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Common-mode failure</td>\n<td>Single cause capable of affecting multiple nominally redundant components or sources.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Graceful degradation</td>\n<td>Controlled reduction of capability as source quality or availability falls.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Holdover</td>\n<td>Continued timing operation after loss of an external reference, with uncertainty that grows over time.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Threat model</td>\n<td>Defined set of threat conditions, capabilities and assumptions against which a system is assessed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Recovery</td>\n<td>Controlled process of restoring and revalidating trusted PNT operation after disruption/manipulation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Threat / adverse condition</td>\n<td>An event, condition or actor capable of disrupting or manipulating a PNT-dependent system.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Vulnerability</td>\n<td>A weakness, exposure or dependency that allows a threat or adverse condition to cause PNT harm.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Supply-chain dependency</td>\n<td>Reliance on third-party hardware, software, services or upstream infrastructure that can affect PNT trust or availability.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "further-learning", "title": "Further Learning", "html": "<p><strong>UK Government: Positioning, Navigation and Timing Overview</strong><br/>\nNational PNT Office guidance on UK PNT dependency, resilience, interference and contingency planning.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/positioning-navigation-and-timing-overview\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gov.uk/guidance/positioning-navigation-and-timing-overview</a></p>\n<p><strong>UK Space Strategy</strong><br/>\nCurrent UK strategy discussing jamming, spoofing, space weather, cyber threats and layered resilient PNT.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/uk-space-strategy/uk-space-strategy\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/uk-space-strategy/uk-space-strategy</a></p>\n<p><strong>UK Government: Space-based PNT Technical Concepts</strong><br/>\nTechnical discussion of resilience, jamming, spoofing, authentication, diversity and alternative PNT concepts.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/space-based-pnt-technical-concepts/space-based-pnt-technical-concepts\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/space-based-pnt-technical-concepts/space-based-pnt-technical-concepts</a></p>\n<p><strong>NIST: Foundational PNT Profile, Revision 2 Draft</strong><br/>\n2026 risk-management profile applying the Cybersecurity Framework to systems using PNT services.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/ir/8323/r2/ipd\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/ir/8323/r2/ipd</a></p>\n<p><strong>NIST: Responsible Use of PNT Services</strong><br/>\nPNT cybersecurity and resilience resources covering disruption, manipulation and risk-informed use.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.nist.gov/pnt\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.nist.gov/pnt</a></p>\n<p><strong>UK MOD: Developing a Robust Global Navigation System for UK Defence</strong><br/>\nUK Defence context for assured PNT and resilience to electromagnetic interference.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/robust-global-navigation-system-project-to-deliver-a-precise-source-of-position-velocity-and-time-information-for-defence\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gov.uk/guidance/robust-global-navigation-system-project-to-deliver-a-precise-source-of-position-velocity-and-time-information-for-defence</a></p>\n<p><strong>UK Space Strategy: Technical Delivery Annex</strong><br/>\nCurrent UK plans for PNT interference monitoring and warning, LEO PNT, quantum PNT and resilient timing.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/uk-space-strategy/uk-space-strategy-technical-delivery-annex\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/uk-space-strategy/uk-space-strategy-technical-delivery-annex</a></p>\n<p><strong>GPS.gov: GPS Interference</strong><br/>\nUS government background on GPS interference, jamming and spoofing risks.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gps.gov/gps-threats\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gps.gov/gps-threats</a></p>\n<h2 id=\"m06-dhs-cisa-resilient-pnt-conformance-framework-v2.0\">DHS / CISA: Resilient PNT Conformance Framework v2.0</h2>\n<p>Outcome-based framework for resilient PNT user equipment, including prevent/respond/recover behaviours and resilience levels.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.dhs.gov/sites/default/files/2022-05/22_0531_st_resilient_pnt_conformance_framework_v2.0.pdf\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.dhs.gov/sites/default/files/2022-05/22_0531_st_resilient_pnt_conformance_framework_v2.0.pdf</a></p>\n<h2 id=\"m06-rethinkpnt-pnt-system-resilience\">RethinkPNT: PNT System Resilience</h2>\n<p>Structured treatment of PNT threats, resilience functions, requirements, techniques, verification and assurance.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://rethinkpnt.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/Website_copy_PNT-System-Resilience.pdf\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://rethinkpnt.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/Website_copy_PNT-System-Resilience.pdf</a></p>\n<h2 id=\"m06-royal-institute-of-navigation-pnt-resilience-checklist\">Royal Institute of Navigation: PNT Resilience Checklist</h2>\n<p>Concise organisational checklist covering dependencies, independent sources, extended outages, mitigation and resilience testing.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://rin.org.uk/mpage/Resilient_PNT_Checklist\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://rin.org.uk/mpage/Resilient_PNT_Checklist</a></p>\n<h2 id=\"m06-royal-institute-of-navigation-impacts-of-gnss-interference-on-maritime-safety-2026\">Royal Institute of Navigation: Impacts of GNSS Interference on Maritime Safety (2026)</h2>\n<p>Applied evidence on interference, spoofing, detection, vessel-system dependencies, operational mitigations and complementary PNT.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://rin.org.uk/general/custom.asp?page=RIN_Maritime_Report\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://rin.org.uk/general/custom.asp?page=RIN_Maritime_Report</a></p>"}, {"id": "next-module", "title": "Next Module", "html": "<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">MODULE 07 — RESILIENT PNT ENGINEERING</p><div class=\"callout-body\"></div></aside>\n<p>Module 7 turns the threats in this module into architecture. It will cover redundancy, diversity and dissimilarity; source independence; sensor fusion and integrity monitoring; holdover and bridging; graceful degradation; detection and isolation; recovery; resilient timing; and designing a PNT system-of-systems around measurable operational requirements.</p>"}], "practice": [{"n": 1, "prompt": "Which best distinguishes jamming from spoofing?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Jamming primarily denies/degrades reception; spoofing attempts to make false PNT information appear credible."}, {"id": "B", "text": "They are two names for exactly the same failure."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Spoofing always causes immediate loss of all satellites."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Jamming only affects timing."}], "answer": ["A"], "rationale": "Jamming targets availability/reception; spoofing targets trust by introducing misleading information."}, {"n": 2, "prompt": "Why can misleading PNT be more dangerous than obvious loss of PNT?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "It always improves accuracy."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Systems and operators may continue acting on plausible but incorrect information."}, {"id": "C", "text": "It cannot affect automation."}, {"id": "D", "text": "It is easier to detect."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "A plausible wrong solution can continue to influence decisions and automated behaviour."}, {"n": 3, "prompt": "What is meaconing?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "A receiver measuring magnetic north."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Reception and rebroadcast/delay of legitimate navigation signals so the victim observes misleading timing/ranging information."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Loss of power to a receiver."}, {"id": "D", "text": "An ionospheric correction."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "Meaconing alters the effective propagation context of otherwise legitimate signals."}, {"n": 4, "prompt": "Which can produce PNT disruption without a hostile RF attack?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Faulty electronics causing interference."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Space weather."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Antenna/cable failure."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Bad configuration or software."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Correction-service outage."}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "rationale": "PNT failure can be natural, accidental, technical or service-related as well as hostile."}, {"n": 5, "prompt": "Which statement about authentication is most accurate?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Authentication alone proves every received range is correct."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Authentication can help establish source/data legitimacy but may not by itself detect delayed or rebroadcast authentic signals."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Authentication makes receivers immune to jamming."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Authentication removes the need for independent sources."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "Authentication addresses source/data legitimacy but does not automatically prove propagation path or freshness."}, {"n": 6, "prompt": "Which may indicate GNSS deception or degradation?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Disagreement with independent inertial/odometry information."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Implausible motion relative to physical constraints."}, {"id": "C", "text": "GNSS time disagreement with an independent clock."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Receiver integrity/interference alarms."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Unusual correlated changes across measurements."}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "rationale": "Detection is strongest when multiple independent indicators are considered together."}, {"n": 7, "prompt": "Which is an example of common-mode vulnerability?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Two GNSS receivers using the same antenna and power supply."}, {"id": "B", "text": "A GNSS receiver and a completely independent mechanical clock having no shared infrastructure."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Two unrelated maps stored separately."}, {"id": "D", "text": "A platform having more than one sensor."}], "answer": ["A"], "rationale": "Separate receivers do not provide full redundancy when they share a component whose failure can affect both."}, {"n": 8, "prompt": "What should happen in a mature PNT risk lifecycle?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Identify dependencies and risks."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Protect critical components and architecture."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Detect disruption/manipulation."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Respond safely."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Recover and re-establish trust."}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "rationale": "Resilience requires risk understanding, protection, detection, safe response and controlled recovery."}, {"n": 9, "prompt": "A receiver loses GNSS near a piece of industrial equipment. What can you conclude immediately?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "A hostile jammer is definitely present."}, {"id": "B", "text": "An interference/reception problem may exist, but cause and intent require further evidence."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The satellites have all failed."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Spoofing is proven."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "Observed interference does not establish intent or root cause without supporting evidence."}, {"n": 10, "prompt": "Which questions strengthen an 'assured PNT' claim?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "What threat model was tested?"}, {"id": "B", "text": "How quickly is misleading information detected?"}, {"id": "C", "text": "What independent fallback sources exist?"}, {"id": "D", "text": "What performance remains in degraded mode?"}, {"id": "E", "text": "What evidence demonstrates recovery and revalidation?"}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "rationale": "Assurance claims need bounded threats, measurable behaviour and supporting evidence."}]});
